<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albertus Magnus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1200–1280</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Albert the Great, Saint Albert</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albertus Magnus was a German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a>, philosopher and scientist, teacher of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a>, who did much to bring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s works into Christian learning. He served briefly as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Regensburg from 1260 to 1262 before resigning to return to teaching. He was canonised in 1931 and named a Doctor of the Church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albertus-magnus.jpg" alt="Albertus Magnus" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vicente Salvador Gómez · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vicente_salvador_gomez-san_alberto.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
